<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalabhra dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">250 CE – 600 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rulers of the Tamil country during a poorly documented period between the Sangam age and the rise of the Pallavas and Pandyas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kalabhras are known mainly from later sources hostile to them. The 8th-century Velvikudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> say a Kalabhra king overthrew the Tamil kings and seized the land, ending the rule of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-pandyas">Early Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-cholas">Early Cholas</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-cheras">Early Cheras</a> around the 3rd century. Their origins are unknown; they may have come from the hill country to the north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Very little is known about their rule. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> sources accuse them of suppressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> grants, which may reflect patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>; the Buddhist writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhadatta">Buddhadatta</a> mentions a king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accuta-vikkanta">Accuta Vikkanta</a> of the Kalabhra family in the Kaveri region. Much Tamil ethical literature, possibly including parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirukkural">Tirukkural</a> tradition, is often dated to this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 6th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadungon">Kadungon</a> and the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simhavishnu">Simhavishnu</a> expelled the Kalabhras, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-pandyan-empire">First Pandyan Empire</a> and expanding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalabhra-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kalabhra dynasty" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: Lotlil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_tamilakam_ports.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 CE – 600 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jainism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kalabhras, Kalabhra interregnum</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=425&amp;focus=kalabhra-dynasty" aria-label="Kalabhra dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6878 1762 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalabhra dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7198 2018L7154 2063M7198 2018L7188 1956M7198 2018L7167 2030M7198 2018L7101 2053M7198 2018L7154 2063"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7154 2063h0M7188 1956h0M7167 2030h0M7101 2053h0M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7198 2018h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7198 2018h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=425&amp;focus=kalabhra-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
